syntax:
Constructor with member initializer list: 
ClassName::Constructor(params) : member1(value1), member2(value2) { // Constructor body }

In-class initializer (C++11+): 
class ClassName { 
    Type member1 = defaultValue;  // Provides a default value
    Type member2;  // No initializer, will be default-initialized if possible
};

Practical usage notes: Always initialize all members in the initializer list, especially built-in types, to avoid undefined behavior. If in-class initializers are available, use them for defaults, but explicitly initialize in constructors for specific values. This pattern promotes exception safety and efficiency by minimizing unnecessary assignments.
